= Computer science
{wiki}

Computer science studies how information can be represented and transformed by <algorithms>, and what those transformations can achieve. Its central questions connect <mathematics> to practical design: what answer should a procedure produce, why is it correct, and how much work does it require as its <input> grows? <Computability theory> asks which tasks can be solved at all; <computational complexity theory> asks what resources a solution needs.

The beauty is that choosing a representation can change what is feasible. Finding a name among a million entries sounds like a million checks. Store the entries in a <sorted array>, and <binary search> needs at most twenty middle-entry comparisons. The order makes each unsuccessful comparison eliminate a whole region of possibilities. A <data structure> supplies useful organization; an <algorithm> exploits it; <time complexity> explains the resulting savings.

<Algorithm correctness> covers every permitted <input>, including absent names and empty collections. <Time complexity> predicts scaling; <statistical program profiling> investigates a particular implementation.

\Include[computer-science-author]
\Include[computer-science-bibliography]

= Logic programming
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

= Negation as failure
{parent=Logic programming}
{wiki}

A negative rule premise interpreted through a specified rule semantics rather than as an independently asserted classical negative fact. <Stable model semantics> evaluates such premises through a candidate interpretation and its <stable model reduct>.

= Forward chaining
{parent=Logic programming}
{wiki}

Rule evaluation that repeatedly adds consequences of premises already known to hold. Positive <Datalog> evaluation computes its <least Herbrand model> in this way.

= Backward chaining
{parent=Logic programming}
{wiki}

Goal-directed rule evaluation that replaces a goal by premises of a matching rule. Shared variables require consistent answer <first-order substitutions> across the premises.

= Datalog
{parent=Logic programming}
{c}
{wiki}

A function-free rule language based on definite <first-order Horn clauses>, normally with safety conditions on rule variables. Positive <Datalog> has a <least Herbrand model> obtained by <forward chaining>.

= Disjunctive Datalog
{parent=Datalog}

<Datalog> allowing disjunctions of positive atoms in rule heads. A satisfiable program can have incomparable minimal <Herbrand models> and no <least Herbrand model>.

= Datalog with negation
{parent=Datalog}
{c}
{title2=$\mathrm{Datalog}^{\neg}$}

<Datalog> with negative body premises. Its meaning depends on the specified semantics, such as <stable model semantics> or evaluation of a <stratified Datalog> program.

= Stratified Datalog
{parent=Datalog with negation}

A <Datalog with negation> program whose predicates can be assigned levels so that positive premises do not exceed their head's level and negative premises have strictly lower levels. Finite programs of this form can be evaluated stratum by stratum and have a unique <stable model>.

= Stable model semantics
{parent=Datalog with negation}
{wiki}

A semantics in which an interpretation must equal the <least Herbrand model> of its own <stable model reduct>. Some programs have no <stable model>, and others have several.

= Skeptical entailment
{parent=Stable model semantics}

A consequence holds skeptically if it is true in every admitted model, in particular every <stable model> when using <stable model semantics>. Exhibiting one supporting <stable model> establishes only existence of support, not <skeptical entailment>.

= Stable model reduct
{parent=Stable model semantics}
{title2=$\mathcal K^I$}

Ground the rules, delete every rule having a negative body atom true in the candidate $I$, and remove all remaining negative premises. The result is a positive program whose <least Herbrand model> is compared with $I$.

= Stable model
{parent=Stable model semantics}

A <Herbrand interpretation> equal to the <least Herbrand model> of the positive reduct obtained relative to that interpretation.

= Knowledge base
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

A collection of assertions and rules interpreted together. A logical <knowledge base> can be studied through its models, its consequences, or a specified nonclassical semantics.

= Database
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

An organized collection of data together with rules for its representation and access.

= Entity-relationship model
{parent=Database}
{wiki}

Represents entity types, their identifiers and <database attributes>, and relationships with participation and cardinality constraints.

= Entity subtype
{parent=Entity-relationship model}

An entity type whose instances inherit the identifier, <database attributes> and relationships of a more general entity type. A total disjoint specialization requires every parent instance to belong to exactly one listed subtype.

= Entity-relationship diagram
{parent=Entity-relationship model}

A diagram of an <entity-relationship model>, including relationship cardinalities and, when needed, subtype and identification constraints.

= Database transaction
{parent=Database}

A sequence of operations treated as one unit of <database> work.

= Database schedule
{parent=Database transaction}

An ordering of operations of several <database transactions> that preserves the operation order within each <database transaction>.

= Conflict-serializable database schedule
{parent=Database schedule}

A <database schedule> that can be turned into a <serial database schedule> by swapping adjacent operations of different <database transactions> that do not conflict. Reads and writes of the same object conflict when at least one is a write.

= Database precedence graph
{parent=Conflict-serializable database schedule}

A <directed graph> with a vertex for each <database transaction> and an edge from the earlier to the later participant in each conflicting pair of operations. A <database schedule> is a <conflict-serializable database schedule> exactly when this <directed graph> has no directed cycle.

= Serial database schedule
{parent=Database schedule}

A <database schedule> in which every <database transaction> runs as one contiguous group, without interleaving another <database transaction>.

= Database page
{parent=Database}

A block transferred between storage and memory; <database query plan> costs are often estimated by the number of such transfers.

= Database query
{parent=Database}

= Database query plan
{parent=Database query}

A physical recipe for evaluating a <database query>, specifying scans, index accesses and implementations of <relational joins> and duplicate elimination.

= Block nested-loop join
{parent=Database query plan}

A <relational join> implementation that retains a block of outer records in memory while scanning the inner input. With $b$ outer pages per block, $p$ outer pages and $q$ inner pages, its data-read cost is $p+\lceil p/b\rceil q$, before temporary output costs.

= Clustered database index
{parent=Database query plan}

An index whose search order determines, or closely follows, the physical placement of the data records. Clustering by one <database attribute> does not give indexed access by an unrelated <database attribute>.

= Database aggregation
{parent=Database query}

Computes a summary such as a count, sum or <arithmetic mean> over a collection of records.

= Bag semantics
{parent=Database query}

<Database query> results may contain repeated records, with their multiplicities retained.

= Set semantics
{parent=Database query}

<Database query> results contain no repeated <database tuples>. SQL usually uses <bag semantics> unless duplicate elimination is requested.

= Database query language
{parent=Database query}

= Domain relational calculus
{parent=Database query language}
{wiki}

A <database query language> using <first-order logic> variables for individual values rather than whole <database tuples>.

= Database query safety
{parent=Domain relational calculus}

The requirement that a <database query> have a finite, appropriately range-restricted answer on finite input. In the usual domain-independent interpretation, extending the ambient value domain with unused values must not change the answer. The absence of a negation symbol alone does not ensure safety.

= Domain independence of a database query
{parent=Database query safety}

A <database query> has the same result when its ambient domain is extended while all input <database relations> are unchanged.

= SQL
{c}
{parent=Database query language}
{wiki}

= SQL NULL
{c}
{parent=SQL}

SQL's marker for an unknown or absent value. <Database aggregation> generally ignores these markers, and `AVG` returns this marker when there are no non-null inputs.<Aggregate Functions in the PostgreSQL documentation>[Section 9.21, general-purpose aggregate functions and empty-input behavior]{ref}

= Relational algebra
{parent=Database query language}
{wiki}

An algebra of operations on <database relations>. Classical <relational algebra> has <relational selection>, <relational projection>, <relational join>, <Cartesian product>, union and <relational difference>; <database aggregation> requires an extension.

= Relational division
{parent=Relational algebra}

A <relational algebra> operation expressing a universal matching requirement. An explicit <relational difference> construction can specify the intended candidate universe, including the case of an empty divisor.

= Relational difference
{parent=Relational algebra}

For two <database relations> with compatible <database attributes>, retains the <database tuples> in the first and absent from the second.

= Relational join
{parent=Relational algebra}
{title2=$\bowtie$}

Combines <database tuples> satisfying a matching condition. A <natural join> uses equality of all shared <database attribute> names.

= Natural join
{parent=Relational join}

= Relational projection
{parent=Relational algebra}
{title2=$\pi$}

Retains specified <database attributes>, eliminating repeated <database tuples> under <set semantics>.

= Relational selection
{parent=Relational algebra}
{title2=$\sigma$}

Retains the <database tuples> satisfying a condition.

= Relational database
{parent=Database}
{wiki}

A <database> organized using the <relational model>.

= Relational model
{parent=Relational database}
{wiki}

Data are represented as <database relations> whose <database tuples> have named <database attributes>.

= Relational schema
{parent=Relational model}

Names of <database relations>, their <database attributes> and value domains, together with applicable <database integrity constraints>.

= Database normalization
{parent=Relational schema}
{wiki}

= Dependency-preserving decomposition
{parent=Database normalization}

A decomposition in which the <database integrity constraints> can be enforced using constraints on individual component <database relations>, without computing their <relational join>.

= Lossless-join decomposition
{parent=Database normalization}
{wiki}

A decomposition of a <relational schema> whose component <relational projections> always reconstruct the original <database relation> by <natural join>, without extra <database tuples>.

= Fourth normal form
{parent=Database normalization}
{wiki}

= 4NF
{c}
{synonym}

A <relational schema> is in <fourth normal form> if the determinant of every non-trivial <multivalued dependency> is a <superkey>. This implies <Boyce-Codd normal form> because every <functional dependency> implies a <multivalued dependency>.

= Boyce-Codd normal form
{c}
{parent=Database normalization}
{wiki=Boyce–Codd_normal_form}

= BCNF
{c}
{synonym}

A <relational schema> is in <Boyce-Codd normal form> if the determinant of every non-trivial <functional dependency> is a <superkey>.

= Multivalued dependency
{parent=Relational schema}
{wiki}
{title2=$X\twoheadrightarrow Y$}

= MVD
{c}
{synonym}

For <database tuples> agreeing on $X$, requires the combinations obtained by swapping their values on $Y\setminus X$ and on the remaining <database attributes> to occur too. A <functional dependency> implies the corresponding <multivalued dependency>; complementation exchanges $Y$ with the remaining <database attributes>. A <multivalued dependency> is trivial if $Y\subseteq X$ or $X\cup Y$ is the full <relational schema>.

= Functional dependency
{parent=Relational schema}
{wiki}
{title2=$X\to Y$}

= FD
{c}
{synonym}

A <database integrity constraint> saying that any two <database tuples> agreeing on <database attributes> $X$ also agree on <database attributes> $Y$.

= Attribute closure
{parent=Functional dependency}
{title2=$X^+$}

The set of <database attributes> determined by $X$ under given <functional dependencies>. Start from $X$ and repeatedly add the right-hand side of each <functional dependency> whose left-hand side is already included.

= Foreign key
{parent=Relational schema}
{wiki}

A <database integrity constraint> requiring values of specified <database attributes> to occur in a referenced identifier of another <database relation>.

= Superkey
{parent=Relational schema}
{wiki}

A set of <database attributes> that determines every <database attribute> of a <database relation> under its <database integrity constraints>.

= Candidate key
{parent=Superkey}
{wiki}

An inclusion-minimal <superkey>: removing any of its <database attributes> destroys the <superkey> property. Minimal means inclusion-minimal, not necessarily smallest in size.

= Database primary key
{parent=Candidate key}

The <candidate key> selected as the principal identifier for <database tuples> of a <database relation>.

= Database integrity constraint
{parent=Relational schema}

A condition required of every legal instance of a <relational schema>.

= Database attribute
{parent=Relational model}

A named component of a <database tuple>.

= Database tuple
{parent=Relational model}

One record of a <database relation>, assigning a value to each <database attribute>.

= Database relation
{parent=Relational model}

A <set> of <database tuples> with the same named <database attributes>; unlike an SQL result without duplicate elimination, a mathematical <database relation> has no repeated <database tuples>.

= Computer architecture
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

The organization and behavior of computing systems, including their <instruction set architecture>, <CPU> execution machinery and <computer memory> interfaces.

= Floating-point unit
{parent=Computer architecture}
{wiki}

Hardware for <floating-point arithmetic>. Its cost and historical absence on some processors motivate <integer arithmetic> implementations of <raster graphics> algorithms.

= Unicode
{c}
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

= Data redundancy
{parent=Computer science}

Storing information that is also derivable from other stored information. A <hard link> count summarizes <directory entries>, while a <block allocation bitmap> summarizes block ownership in a <file system>. Such summaries accelerate common operations and permit consistency comparisons; replicated data can additionally assist recovery. Every update must maintain the redundant relationship, or establish a protocol for detecting and repairing divergence.

= Concurrent computing
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

= Communicating Scala Objects
{c}
{parent=Concurrent computing}

A <Scala> library for constructing communicating concurrent processes with typed <message-passing channels>, parallel composition and <guarded alternation>.<Communicating Scala Objects by Bernard Sufrin>[§§1–5]{ref}

= Guarded alternation
{parent=Communicating Scala Objects}

= Alternation
{synonym}

A choice among enabled communication events, committing one ready event. An event whose Boolean guard is false is not offered. Output events must be selected before their value-producing expression or post-send state update is performed.

= Process algebra
{parent=Concurrent computing}
{wiki}

A <process algebra> describes interacting systems through <CSP events>, composition operators and equations relating their behaviours. <Communicating sequential processes> is one such framework.

= Communicating sequential processes
{parent=Process algebra}
{wiki}

= CSP
{c}
{synonym}

A <process algebra> in which components communicate through synchronized <CSP events>. Its operational description uses a <labelled transition system>; standard observations include <CSP traces>, <CSP stable failures> and <CSP divergences>.<FDR2 User Manual>[§1.2, The CSP View of the World, opening paragraphs and list of semantic models]{ref}

= CSP operational semantics
{parent=Communicating sequential processes}
{c}

Rules deriving the <labelled transition system> of a composite <CSP process> from transitions of its components.

= CSP step law
{parent=CSP operational semantics}
{c}

An algebraic expansion listing the initially possible <CSP events> and their continuations. It translates operational transition cases into <CSP external choice> expressions.

= Unique fixed point principle
{parent=Communicating sequential processes}
{title2=$P=F(P),\ Q=F(Q)\Rightarrow P=Q$}

For a suitably <guarded recursion>, two solutions of the same defining equation denote the same <CSP process>. There is also a mutually recursive version, comparing tuples of solutions.

= CSP semantic model
{parent=Communicating sequential processes}
{c}

A representation of a <CSP process> by specified kinds of observable behaviour. The <CSP traces model>, <CSP stable-failures model> and <CSP failures-divergences model> record progressively more information about refusals and internal activity.

= CSP refinement
{parent=CSP semantic model}
{c}
{title2=$S\sqsubseteq_M I$}

Implementation $I$ has only behaviours permitted by specification $S$ in <CSP semantic model> $M$. In particular, <CSP traces model> refinement means $\operatorname{traces}(I)\subseteq\operatorname{traces}(S)$; the other models additionally constrain <CSP stable failures> or <CSP divergences>.

= CSP failures-divergences model
{parent=CSP semantic model}
{c}
{title2=$FD$}

Record <CSP stable failures> and <CSP divergences>, treating behaviour after a <CSP divergence> as unrestricted. This can detect both stable refusal of progress and endless internal activity.

= CSP stable-failures model
{parent=CSP semantic model}
{c}
{title2=$F$}

Represent a <CSP process> by its <CSP traces> and <CSP stable failures>.

= CSP traces model
{parent=CSP semantic model}
{c}
{title2=$T$}

Represent a <CSP process> by its set of <CSP traces>.

= CSP divergence
{parent=CSP semantic model}
{c}

The possibility, after a finite visible <CSP trace>, of an infinite sequence of consecutive <CSP internal actions>. The <CSP failures-divergences model> records this possibility explicitly.<FDR2 User Manual>[§1.2, failures/divergences model bullet]{ref}

= CSP stable failure
{parent=CSP semantic model}
{c}
{title2=$(s,X)$}

After <CSP trace> $s$, the <CSP process> can reach a <CSP stable state> offering no event in the refusal set $X$. Refusal is existential over reachable stable alternatives.<FDR2 User Manual>[§1.2, stable failures model bullet]{ref}

= CSP trace
{parent=CSP semantic model}
{c}
{title2=$s\in\operatorname{traces}(P)$}

A finite <sequence> of visible <CSP events> performed by a <CSP process>, omitting <CSP internal actions>.

= CSP process
{parent=Communicating sequential processes}
{c}
{title2=$P$}

A component specified by possible <CSP events> and successor behaviours. $\mathrm{STOP}$ has no transitions. <CSP prefixing>, <CSP choice> and <CSP parallel composition> construct larger components.

= CSP exception
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$P\mathbin\Theta_AQ$}

Behave as $P$ until it performs a visible <CSP event> in $A$, then switch to $Q$. The triggering event is included in the visible <CSP trace>.

= CSP interrupt
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$P\mathbin\triangle Q$}

Allow $P$ to proceed while $Q$ is ready to interrupt. The first visible <CSP event> of $Q$ discards $P$ and leaves the derivative of $Q$. A <CSP internal action> of $Q$ alone does not discard $P$.

= CSP hiding
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$P\setminus A$}

Turn <CSP events> in $A$ into <CSP internal actions>. Infinite hidden activity can create <CSP divergence>.

= CSP event renaming
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$f(P)$}

Relabel visible <CSP events> according to a function or relation, preserving <CSP internal actions>. An <injective> relabelling preserves event identity; a non-injective one can merge distinct actions. In relational renaming, an original event with no image is disabled. Thus partial relational renaming can also restrict an interface.

= CSP parallel composition
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$P\underset A\Vert Q$}

Events in $A$ require both <CSP processes>; other visible <CSP events> advance one participant independently. <CSP internal actions> do not synchronize.

= CSP alphabetized parallel composition
{parent=CSP parallel composition}
{c}
{title2=$P\ {}_A\Vert_B\ Q$}

The alphabets assigned to participants determine their shared <CSP events>. An event in both assigned alphabets requires both participants; one assigned only to a single participant advances that participant. Replicated composition synchronizes all participants whose assigned alphabets contain the event.

= CSP choice
{parent=CSP process}
{c}

= CSP internal choice
{parent=CSP choice}
{c}
{title2=$P\sqcap Q$}

The process may internally choose either branch, independently of the environment's offered <CSP events>. Consequently it can have more <CSP stable failures> than the corresponding <CSP external choice>, despite having the same <CSP traces>.

= CSP external choice
{parent=CSP choice}
{c}
{title2=$P\mathbin\square Q$}

The first visible <CSP event> resolves the choice; the environment can select an event offered by only one branch. <CSP internal actions> can advance a branch without selecting it.

= CSP prefixing
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$a\to P$}

Perform <CSP event> $a$, then behave as <CSP process> $P$.

= CSP internal action
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$\tau$}

An unobservable transition that the environment cannot block. <CSP hiding> turns selected visible <CSP events> into <CSP internal actions>.

= CSP stable state
{parent=CSP internal action}
{c}

A state with no outgoing <CSP internal action>. A <CSP stable failure> records a refusal only after such a state has been reached.

= CSP event
{parent=CSP process}
{c}
{title2=$a\in\Sigma$}

An instantaneous visible interaction. A channel event such as $\mathit{left}.x$ combines a channel name with a communicated value. Participants synchronize on one event rather than separately sending and receiving it.

= Thread safety
{parent=Concurrent computing}

A property of a component whose specified behavior remains correct when operations are invoked concurrently by several threads under the stated synchronization assumptions.

= Computer security
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

= Cybersecurity
{synonym}

= Proof of work
{parent=Computer security}
{wiki}

A moderately expensive computation whose result can be checked cheaply. Request-specific puzzles can mitigate a computational <denial-of-service attack> if verification precedes expensive service and solved puzzles cannot be reused indefinitely.

= Denial-of-service attack
{parent=Computer security}
{wiki}

An attack on <service availability> by exhausting resources or preventing useful work. Requiring inexpensive unauthenticated messages to trigger expensive private-key operations creates an amplification opportunity.

= Replay attack
{parent=Computer security}
{wiki}

Reuse of previously observed valid messages to obtain an acceptance or action in a different context. <Cryptographic nonces>, fresh <session keys> and explicit context binding can prevent particular replay patterns.

= Preplay attack
{parent=Replay attack}

Obtaining valid responses to a predicted future challenge before that challenge is issued, then delivering them when the predicted value becomes current. Challenge uniqueness alone need not establish freshness when challenges are predictable.

= Trusted third party
{parent=Computer security}
{wiki}

A participant whose correct behaviour and protection of secret state are assumptions of a security argument. It can mediate <authentication> and <session key transport>, but the <protocol> still needs to authenticate its responses.

= Data integrity
{parent=Computer security}
{wiki}

Protection of data against unauthorised or accidental alteration. It is distinct from <information confidentiality> and <service availability>.

= Information confidentiality
{parent=Computer security}

Protection against unauthorised disclosure of information. <Access control> can prevent a subject from reading a file, while <cryptographic confidentiality> protects message contents against specified adversaries using <cryptography>.

= Declassification
{parent=Information confidentiality}

An authorised release of information from a more restrictive protection level to a less restrictive one. Its policy must identify who may release which information, rather than treating every downward flow as acceptable.

= Access control
{parent=Computer security}
{wiki}

Rules and mechanisms deciding which subjects may perform which operations on protected objects. <Discretionary access control> permits designated owners to grant rights; the <Bell-LaPadula model> instead constrains information flow by classification.

= Readwrite
{parent=Access control}

An access right combining reading and writing, as opposed to a blind write that reveals no existing contents. Its precise meaning is part of the access model.

= Bell-LaPadula model
{c}
{parent=Access control}
{wiki=Bell–LaPadula_model}

A classification-based <information confidentiality> model restricting subject accesses to prevent forbidden information flow. The <simple security property> restricts reading above clearance; the <Bell-LaPadula star property> restricts information flow through current reads and writes. It is not a complete <data integrity> policy.

= Bell-LaPadula star property
{c}
{parent=Bell-LaPadula model}

The <Bell-LaPadula model> restriction that a subject operating at its current level reads only at or below that level and writes only at or above it. An access combining both operations therefore requires equal levels. Safe changes of current level must also control retained information.

= Simple security property
{parent=Bell-LaPadula model}
{title2=$f_O(o)\le f_S(s)$}

The <Bell-LaPadula model> restriction that a readable object's classification must not exceed the subject's maximum clearance.

= Discretionary access control
{parent=Access control}
{wiki}

= Authentication
{parent=Computer security}
{wiki}

= Bilateral authentication
{parent=Authentication}

= Mutual authentication
{synonym}

An <authentication> goal in which both participants establish the identity and relevant participation of the other. Freshness, agreement on parameters and completed participation are separate properties that a <protocol> should specify.

= Identity provider
{parent=Authentication}
{wiki}

= Distributed system
{parent=Computer science}

A <distributed system> contains communicating computing components whose coordination crosses machine boundaries. Distribution can improve capacity or <fault tolerance>, but it introduces communication and partial-failure problems. The physical placement of components does not by itself determine who controls them.

= Termination detection
{parent=Distributed system}

A <protocol> for detecting that a distributed computation has completed. Merely observing each participant idle at different instants need not establish a simultaneous idle state; the <protocol> must account for intervening changes and communications.

= Hard state
{parent=Distributed system}

<Hard state> persists until explicitly changed or deleted. The distinction from <soft state> concerns maintenance and recovery rules rather than simply whether the storage is volatile.

= Soft state
{parent=Distributed system}

<Soft state> is maintained through refreshes and expires when they cease. Continuing participants can rebuild it through later communication, trading refresh overhead and temporary inconsistency for automatic cleanup and recovery.

= Mandatory service dependency
{parent=Distributed system}
{title2=$u\to v$}

Fix the operation being considered and the conditions under which it must complete. A <mandatory service dependency> $u\to v$ means that completing the designated operation of $u$ requires $v$ to supply its designated service. Dependencies can be represented by a <directed graph>. The operation and conditions matter: an operation that can use an already retained result need not require a new response from its original source.

A <directed path> of mandatory dependencies propagates necessity. This follows by <mathematical induction> on the path length: one edge is the definition, and composing one further necessary condition gives the induction step. Consequently, if every replica requires the same component, losing that component can block the operation at every replica. Keeping copies of other components does not remove the shared requirement. Conversely, the availability of every dependency is not by itself a proof that an operation will complete.

= Client-server model
{parent=Distributed system}

In the <client-server model>, a <network client> requests a service supplied by a <network server>. It can be used within a federated deployment or a centralised one; it does not by itself determine whether an application respects the <end-to-end principle>.

= Network server
{parent=Client-server model}

= Server
{synonym}

A <network server> supplies a service to <clients>. Its role is logical rather than necessarily a single machine or location.

= Network client
{parent=Client-server model}

= Client
{synonym}

A <network client> initiates requests for a service. In an encrypted <protocol> it may be a cryptographic <endpoint> even though the service implementation has several further internal components.

= Data portability
{parent=Distributed system}

<Data portability> permits moving useful application data between implementations or providers. An export format alone need not reproduce a provider's interfaces, credentials or service behaviour, so portability and service interoperability are distinct design questions.

= Fault tolerance
{parent=Distributed system}

<Fault tolerance> is the ability to continue satisfying a specified service property despite a stated class of component failures. The failure model and the property matter: preserving stored bytes, accepting new connections and finishing an operation are different requirements.

= Service availability
{parent=Fault tolerance}
{title2=$A=T_{\mathrm{up}}/T$}

For an observation period of duration $T>0$, let $T_{\mathrm{up}}$ be the total time during which a service meets a specified condition. Its measured <service availability> is $A=T_{\mathrm{up}}/T$. The condition must say which operation can be performed: accepting a new user and serving an already admitted user can have different dependencies. A <mandatory service dependency> can reduce <service availability> even when all data copies remain intact.

= Failure domain
{parent=Fault tolerance}
{wiki}

A <failure domain> groups components that can be affected by a specified shared failure. The relevant grouping can follow physical infrastructure or an administrative dependency. Distinct sites do not establish independent behaviour when they still depend on the same component or decision. The grouping must therefore be stated relative to the failures under consideration, rather than inferred from distance alone.

= Data replication
{parent=Distributed system}

<Data replication> maintains multiple copies of data. Its value for <fault tolerance> depends on failure independence, update behaviour and other service dependencies; several copies can still share one operator or a common point of failure.

= Cloud computing
{parent=Distributed system}

<Cloud computing> offers remotely supplied computing resources and services through a service interface. It can make capacity easier to obtain while moving operational and state dependencies to a provider. A distributed physical implementation can remain centrally controlled.

\Include[computer-network]{parent=Computer science}

= Instruction set architecture
{parent=Computer science}

The programmer-visible machine instruction repertoire and its semantics, including accessible <processor registers> and permitted operand forms. Different processors can implement the same <instruction set architecture> using different internal designs.

= POWER architecture
{parent=Instruction set architecture}
{c}

The POWER processor architecture family, whose shared-memory ordering uses dependency rules and barriers such as `sync`, `lwsync` and `isync`. The relevant ordering examples are documented in <Understanding POWER Multiprocessors (2011)>.

= Zero-address instruction
{parent=Instruction set architecture}

An instruction whose encoding contains no explicit operand addresses. A <stack machine> obtains operands implicitly from its operand <stack>, but absence of explicit addresses does not logically force that design: fixed implicit <processor registers> are another possibility. A zero-address indirect-load instruction can still obtain an arbitrary <memory address> from the <stack>; “zero-address” describes the instruction's encoding, not the absence of address-valued data.

= Addressing mode
{parent=Instruction set architecture}

An <addressing mode> specifies how an instruction obtains an operand or forms an effective memory address. Examples include <immediate operands>, <processor register> operands, base-plus-displacement memory access and scaled <processor register> indexing.

= Immediate operand
{parent=Addressing mode}

An <immediate operand> is a value encoded in an instruction rather than fetched from a <processor register> or a separate memory location. Its width and interpretation limit the available values; scaling an encoded field can trade address granularity for displacement range.

= Byte-lane immediate
{parent=Immediate operand}

An <instruction encoding> that stores an eight-bit byte and a two-bit byte-position selector, expanding them to a 32-bit <machine word> as $b2^{8j}$ for $0\leq j\leq3$. Four such values can construct any 32-bit constant using <bitwise OR> or addition <modulo> $2^{32}$.

= Zero extension
{parent=Immediate operand}
{title2=$\operatorname{zext}_{m\to w}(b)$}

Widen an $m$-<bit> <binary representation> to $w>m$ <bits> by retaining the original low positions and filling every new high position with zero. This preserves the <unsigned interpretation>. A <MIPS instruction> such as `ori` uses <zero extension> of its <immediate operand>, whereas `addi` uses <sign extension>.

= Scaled index addressing
{parent=Addressing mode}
{title2=$B+2^kI$}

= Scaled register indexing
{synonym}

<Scaled index addressing> forms a memory address $B+sI$ from a base $B$ and index $I$. For element size $s=2^k$, a <barrel shifter> forms the displacement by a <left bit shift> before the <arithmetic logic unit> adds the base. This directly implements <array> indexing without a separate multiplication instruction.

= SP-relative addressing
{parent=Addressing mode}
{c}

= Stack-pointer-relative addressing
{synonym}

<SP-relative addressing> adds an encoded displacement to the <stack pointer>. Scaling an <immediate operand> by a power of two reaches farther with the same field width but restricts the address grid. An eight-bit nonnegative field scaled by four reaches $0,4,\ldots,1020$, whereas scaling it by sixteen reaches $0,16,\ldots,4080$; it does not provide every intervening byte address.

= Machine instruction
{parent=Instruction set architecture}

A machine-encoded operation executed by a processor. An <assembly language> mnemonic can denote one <machine instruction> or an assembler expansion into several.

= Conditional execution
{parent=Machine instruction}

An instruction changes architectural state only if a specified predicate holds. With <register renaming>, a false conditional write must preserve the preceding logical destination value and satisfy younger consumers of that version.

= Store instruction
{parent=Machine instruction}

A <machine instruction> that writes data to <computer memory>. A <store buffer> can separate local completion from visibility to other <CPU cores>.

= Load instruction
{parent=Machine instruction}

A <machine instruction> that reads a value from <computer memory> into a <processor register>. A demand <cache miss> may delay its result; a <software prefetch> has no such architectural result.

= Instruction encoding
{parent=Machine instruction}

The arrangement of <bits> specifying an operation, its operands and any immediate constants. A fixed-width encoding has a finite budget shared between these fields.

= Opcode
{parent=Instruction encoding}

An operation-selecting field of a <machine instruction>. A primary <opcode> can select a family whose individual operations are distinguished by secondary function fields.

= MIPS architecture
{parent=Instruction set architecture}
{c}

A family of reduced instruction set computer architectures. The classic 32-<bit> <integer> encoding has five-<bit> <processor register> fields, while many branch and jump instructions execute a <branch delay slot>. These conventions differ from later revisions and must be stated when presenting <assembly language>.

= MIPS instruction
{parent=MIPS architecture}
{c}

An instruction in the <MIPS architecture>, with a specified binary encoding and architectural effect. Educational datapaths often simplify details such as <branch delay slots>; instruction behavior in a particular circuit must follow that circuit's stated specification. <MIPS R-type instructions> use register and function fields; <MIPS I-type instructions> use an immediate field as well as register fields.

= Branch delay slot
{parent=MIPS architecture}

In classic delayed-control-transfer MIPS, the instruction immediately after a branch or jump executes before control transfers. A harmless `sll $zero,$zero,0` can fill that slot. A jump-and-link records the address after this slot as its <return address>.<MIPS32 Architecture For Programmers Volume II by MIPS Technologies>[Revision 2.00, JAL entry, p. 139]{ref}

= MIPS I-type instruction
{parent=MIPS architecture}
{c}

The classic 32-<bit> layout is $\mathrm{op}:6,\mathrm{rs}:5,\mathrm{rt}:5,\mathrm{immediate}:16$. The second register field is named $\mathrm{rt}$, including when it denotes a destination; there is no separate $\mathrm{rd}$ field.

= MIPS R-type instruction
{parent=MIPS architecture}
{c}

The classic 32-<bit> layout is $\mathrm{op}:6,\mathrm{rs}:5,\mathrm{rt}:5,\mathrm{rd}:5,\mathrm{shamt}:5,\mathrm{funct}:6$. Not every operation uses every field.

= ARM architecture family
{parent=Instruction set architecture}
{c}

A family of processor architectures with several execution states and instruction encodings. The 32-<bit> instruction sets include the compact <Thumb instruction set>; the 32-<bit> <AAPCS32> <calling convention> specifies interoperation at subroutine boundaries.

= Thumb instruction set
{parent=ARM architecture family}
{c}

A compact instruction encoding for the <ARM architecture family>, with sixteen-bit encodings and, in later versions, additional thirty-two-bit encodings. Operand restrictions depend on the selected architecture. The original sixteen-bit signed halfword load uses a register offset, whereas an ordinary halfword load also offers an immediate offset.<Thumb 16-bit instruction set quick reference by Arm>[Load entries: halfword immediate/register and signed halfword register]{ref}

= Clock edge
{parent=Computer science}

A designated transition of a clock signal at which a <synchronous sequential circuit> updates its stored <bits>. Next-state functions use the state immediately before the edge, and all stored <bits> update together. This convention is essential when composing circuits: a combinational output computed from the old state can be sampled at that edge, while an additional stored output introduces another cycle of delay.

= Reduced instruction set computer
{parent=Computer science}
{title2=RISC}

= RISC architecture
{c}
{synonym}

A processor design using a relatively regular instruction repertoire and explicit restrictions on operand forms. Immediate constants may have limited encodings, so extracting low <bits> with two <bit shifts> can avoid constructing a large <bit mask> in a temporary <processor register>. The benefit depends on the actual instruction set. A compact example appears in the <Thumb 16-bit instruction set quick reference by Arm>, whose logical <bitwise AND> instruction uses a <processor register> operand while shifts accept small immediate counts.

= Competitive programming
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

Competitive programming asks participants to design and implement <algorithms> for specified <computational problems>, usually with constraints on <time complexity> and <space complexity>. A successful solution combines a correctness argument with an implementation that fits the available resources.

= Competitive programming website
{parent=Competitive programming}

\Include[project-euler]

= Quantum computing
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

= Quantum computation
{synonym}

<Quantum computing> processes <quantum information> through controlled physical operations and <quantum measurements>. The <quantum circuit> is one model of such a computation.

= Liquid-state NMR quantum computing
{parent=Quantum computing}

<Liquid-state NMR quantum computing> encodes <qubits> in distinguishable nuclear <spins> of molecules in solution. Selective radiofrequency pulses implement one-spin control; <NMR scalar coupling> supplies entangling evolution. Under $H_J=(\pi\hbar J/2)Z_1Z_2$, evolution for $1/(2J)$ with $J>0$, followed by $-\pi/2$ rotations about each $z$ axis, produces a <controlled-Z gate> up to <global phase>. Ensemble detection and common pseudo-pure preparations make small demonstrations distinct from scalable pure-state initialization.

= Pseudo-pure state
{parent=Liquid-state NMR quantum computing}
{title2=$\rho=(1-p)I/d+p|\psi\rangle\langle\psi|$}

A mixture of the maximally mixed state and a chosen pure state. Unitary conjugation preserves its identity part; every traceless observable has expectation $p\langle\psi|O|\psi\rangle$. The coefficient $p$ is effective polarization rather than literal purity: $\operatorname{tr}\rho^2=1/d+(1-1/d)p^2$. Preserving the largest population of $n$ weakly polarized equal spins and averaging the rest gives $p\simeq n\epsilon/[2(2^n-1)]$, displaying the signal penalty of this high-temperature ensemble preparation.

= Controlled-transfer gate
{parent=Liquid-state NMR quantum computing}
{title2=$c'-d'=\cos\theta(c-d)$}

A controlled <rotation gate> followed by computational-basis <dephasing> mixes the two target populations in the control-one subspace. If they are $c,d$, their sum is preserved and their difference is multiplied by $\cos\theta$. The operation is not unitary after dephasing. On a two-qubit diagonal state, the population of $|00\rangle$ is unchanged when either qubit is the target, because the other control bit is zero.

= DiVincenzo criteria
{parent=Quantum computing}
{c}

The requirements are scalable characterized <qubits>, initialization, long relevant coherence times, a <universal quantum gate set>, and selective readout. They concern the whole experimental information-processing cycle, including resources needed for <quantum error correction>.<The Physical Implementation of Quantum Computation by David P. DiVincenzo>[Section III, requirements 1–5]{ref}

= Universal quantum computation
{parent=Quantum computing}

<Quantum computation> capable of approximating arbitrary finite quantum circuits using a specified <universal quantum gate set>, with an error tolerance and resource scaling.

= Quantum computer
{parent=Quantum computing}

A <quantum computer> is a device that performs <quantum computing>.

\Include[artificial-intelligence]{parent=Computer science}

= Computer
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

= Software
{parent=Computer science}
{wiki}

<Software> consists of instructions and associated data used by a <computer>. A <spreadsheet> is <software> that stores <spreadsheet cells> and evaluates their <spreadsheet formulas>.

= Software engineering
{parent=Software}
{wiki}

= Unified Modeling Language
{c}
{parent=Software engineering}
{wiki}

= UML
{c}
{synonym}

A modeling language for describing software structures and behavior. A <class diagram> records types and relationships; a <sequence diagram> records ordered interactions; a <state diagram> records states and event-triggered transitions.

= State diagram
{parent=Unified Modeling Language}
{wiki}

Describe the possible states of a system and transitions triggered by events, optionally constrained by guards and accompanied by actions. The diagram emphasizes a component's lifecycle and permitted reactions. It can describe many interaction traces, while a <sequence diagram> focuses on the temporal ordering of messages in an interaction.

= Sequence diagram
{parent=Unified Modeling Language}
{wiki}

Describe an interaction through participant lifelines and ordered messages. Time usually proceeds downward. Calls, replies and execution intervals show how participants cooperate in one scenario, with optional fragments for alternatives or repetition. The diagram emphasizes message ordering across participants.

= Class diagram
{parent=Unified Modeling Language}
{wiki}

= UML class diagram
{c}
{synonym}

A structural diagram showing <software classes> or <object interfaces>, their attributes and operations, and relationships such as <class inheritance> and associations. A hollow triangle points toward a more general classifier; a dashed realization line distinguishes implementation of an interface. A plain association does not assert exclusive lifetime ownership of the referenced object.

= Composition in UML
{parent=Class diagram}

A strong whole-part relationship in a <UML class diagram>, conventionally marked by a filled diamond at the whole. It expresses exclusive composite ownership and associated lifetime constraints. A stored reference or observer-registration association alone does not establish composition: the same observer may be registered with several subjects unless the design forbids it.

= Software architecture
{parent=Software engineering}
{wiki}

The organization of a <software> system's major components, their responsibilities and their interactions. A <software architecture> constrains which components own state and which may request or observe changes. <Model-view-controller> is one example for interactive applications.

= Model-view-controller
{parent=Software architecture}
{wiki=Model–view–controller}

= MVC
{c}
{synonym}

Separate an application's domain state and rules into a model, its presentation into a view, and interpretation of user actions into a controller. The controller requests model operations; the view renders coherent model state. A playlist model, for example, owns song order independently of whether a menu, button or keyboard action requests a move. The <Observer pattern> often carries model-change notifications to views, but <model-view-controller> defines responsibilities rather than requiring a particular notification mechanism. This supports <separation of concerns> and testing domain logic independently of a graphical interface.

= Separation of concerns
{parent=Software engineering}
{wiki}

Assign distinct responsibilities to components with explicit interfaces, so one responsibility can change without requiring unrelated changes elsewhere. For example, a <model-view-controller> application separates domain state, presentation and input interpretation. The <Observer pattern> can communicate a coherent state change to several views while keeping concrete display details out of the model. The separation should follow actual responsibilities; multiplying classes without a clear boundary does not establish it.

= Refactoring
{parent=Software}

Change internal software structure while preserving its specified observable behavior. Extracting a coherent operation into a named method or moving a responsibility behind an interface can improve clarity without changing results. Regression tests provide evidence of preservation, while contracts and invariants supply stronger reasoning about the changed structure.

= Code smell
{parent=Refactoring}

An observable feature of source code suggesting a possible underlying design problem. It prompts investigation rather than proving a defect. <Duplicate code>, <feature envy> and a <long method> suggest potential transformations, and context can justify leaving an apparent smell unchanged.<Code Smell by Martin Fowler>[Definition and warning that smells are not automatically bad]{ref}

= Feature envy
{parent=Code smell}

An operation depends heavily on another object's data or responsibilities. Moving the operation, or the relevant coherent part, to that object's abstraction can reduce exposed internals and misplaced coupling. The appropriate home follows responsibility and behavior, not merely the number of field references.<Move Function in the Refactoring catalog>[Moving behavior to the context that serves it better]{ref}

= Long method
{parent=Code smell}

A method whose size or mixture of responsibilities makes its purpose and structure difficult to follow. Extract meaningful steps with clear interfaces, preserving behavior. A fixed line-count threshold is not a proof of poor design; the useful question is whether the method hides coherent separable responsibilities.

= Duplicate code
{parent=Code smell}

Multiple fragments may encode the same decision or responsibility, increasing the risk that a later correction reaches only one copy. A shared operation or component can consolidate genuinely shared knowledge. Independent concepts that merely look similar should not be forced into a common abstraction prematurely.

= Software container
{parent=Software}

A packaged execution environment that reduces differences in software dependencies. It does not establish that the packaged analysis is scientifically correct.

= Version control
{parent=Software}

Recording and identifying changes to files so that a particular computational analysis can be linked to the code that produced it.

= Software bug
{parent=Software}

An implementation defect that makes a program behave contrary to its intended specification. A faithfully implemented but inappropriate scientific model is a different kind of error.

= Software testing
{parent=Software}

= Round-trip testing
{parent=Software testing}

Test a conversion together with its intended inverse. Equality of representations may need normalization, while equality of numerical values can be limited by <floating-point rounding> or truncation. Distinct <strings> can encode the same value, so a value round trip and a character-for-character round trip are different properties.

An independent oracle is still needed: the wrong <functions> $f(x)=x+1$ and $g(y)=y-1$ satisfy both composition identities on <integers>, although neither implements an intended identity conversion. Thus passing inverse-consistency tests does not alone establish the specification of either conversion.

= Equivalence partitioning
{parent=Software testing}

= Equivalence class testing
{synonym}

Select tests by dividing an input domain into classes expected to exercise the same relevant behavior, then choosing representatives, including boundaries. <Integer>-only and point-containing inputs can exercise different paths in a positional-number parser. The classes are part of the test design, not a proof that one representative detects every possible defect in its class. Invalid-format classes need an explicit validation contract before an expected rejection can be asserted.

= White-box testing
{parent=Software testing}

Choose tests using knowledge of internal structure, such as branches, paths or representation cases. Covering all branches is still not generally a proof of correctness for every input. This selection criterion can be combined with specification-based tests within <test-driven development>.

= Black-box testing
{parent=Software testing}

Choose tests from externally specified input/output behaviour without using the implementation's internal control structure as the selection criterion. Boundary cases can be black-box tests when the boundary comes from the contract. Test authorship by the implementer does not alone determine this classification.

= Test-driven development
{parent=Software testing}
{title2=`TDD`}

= TDD
{synonym}
{c}

A development cycle that starts with a small expected-to-fail behavioural test, adds implementation until it passes, and refactors while preserving the tests. Its workflow is distinct from the source of test cases: public-contract tests are black-box tests, while implementation-informed branch cases can be white-box tests. The practice does not by itself prove completeness of the test suite.

= Continuous integration
{parent=Software testing}

Automatically checking changes in a shared software project, commonly by running tests in a recorded environment.

= Metamorphic testing
{parent=Software testing}

Testing relations between outputs for related inputs when an exact reference answer is unavailable. The expected relation must be valid for the scientific problem.

= Property-based testing
{parent=Software testing}

Generating cases and checking general properties such as conservation, symmetry or bounds rather than only selected input-output pairs.

= Regression testing
{parent=Software testing}

= Regression test
{synonym}

Checking whether a software change alters previously established behavior. A reference output must first be justified rather than merely copied from old software.

= Integration testing
{parent=Software testing}

= Integration test
{synonym}

Testing interactions between software components, including data formats, units and ordering.

= Unit testing
{parent=Software testing}

= Unit test
{synonym}

Testing a small software component against a specified expected behavior.

= Test coverage
{parent=Software testing}

